    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d288">

                <pb facs="0463" n="339" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg339"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/3902</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Consul General at Canton</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bergholz</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-06-25"><hi rendition="#italic">June 25,
                        1921</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Department has received a despatch
                    (D.No. 258) of May 7, 1921,<anchor n="14" xml:id="d288-f1"/> signed by Vice
                    Consul Price, reporting upon political conditions in the Canton Consular
                    District, and describing the socalled <pb facs="0464" n="340" xml:id="pg340"/>
                    inauguration of Doctor Sun Yat-sen as President of China. There was enclosed a
                    sealed letter addressed to the President of the United States which the Vice
                    Consul had been asked to transmit. There is nothing in the text of the covering
                    despatch to indicate who the writer of the sealed letter is, or who requested
                    its transmission, but it is assumed that the letter is from Doctor Sun
                    Yat-sen.</p>
                <p>The Department is returning the sealed envelope or letter herewith, and you are
                    instructed to see that it is promptly returned to the sender with a proper oral
                    expression of regret at your inability to forward it.</p>
                <p>It is also desired that you caution Vice Consul Price against the irregularity of
                    permitting the Consulate General to make itself a vehicle of official
                    communication for an organization in revolt against a Government with which the
                    United States is in friendly relations.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Alvey A.
                            Adee</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
